  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a network-based view of brain function across different cognitive states, comparing the normal organization of large-scale brain networks with the effects of brain stimulation. Across the top row, the same distributed set of nodes and connections is shown first without assigning any special state, then reorganized into patterns associated with consciousness, attention, and working memory. Different node colors mark subsets of the network that seem to become more functionally grouped or emphasized for each state, while dashed versus thicker links suggest differences in the strength or importance of long-range interactions.\n\nThe bottom row shows how external brain stimulation, indicated by an arrow aimed at the left frontal side of the brain, can recruit and reshape these networks. In the whole-brain schematic, stimulation activates a cluster of frontal nodes and strengthens several connections. In the simplified network diagrams, stimulation appears to propagate through the system, producing broader integration and stronger pathways that resemble the task-related states above. Overall, the figure conveys that cognition depends on flexible reconfiguration of distributed brain networks, and that stimulation can bias the system toward specific functional network states.",
